[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186124653 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" "question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" "question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" "question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186124655 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/id #uuid "3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/math-academy-id "q-103946" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0 \\\\ -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ -2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-2{x}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" "answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" "answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" "answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" :answer/id #uuid "ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" :answer/id #uuid "0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},1)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" :answer/id #uuid "ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer/id #uuid "3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64" :answer/id #uuid "e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(-\\sqrt{2},-1)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/id #uuid "8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-103926" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4x+8y.$" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2xy+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2xy+8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ 2xy+8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2xy+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{(-2)}=2 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/id #uuid "a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" "answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" "answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" "answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" :answer/id #uuid "372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" :answer/id #uuid "00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ $" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer/id #uuid "21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" :answer/id #uuid "8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8" :answer/id #uuid "769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$(4,-2)$ and $(-4,2)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/id #uuid "c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36677" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(0,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" "answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" "answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" "answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" "answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer/id #uuid "e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" :answer/id #uuid "0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" :answer/id #uuid "39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" :answer/id #uuid "12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3" :answer/id #uuid "002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/id #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36799" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" "answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" "answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" "answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" "answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/id #uuid "1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(-2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/id #uuid "89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/id #uuid "878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(2,2)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/id #uuid "32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "(4,0)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/id #uuid "7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "No stationary points" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/id #uuid "1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-105280" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" "answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" "answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" "answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/id #uuid "cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/id #uuid "8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(1,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/id #uuid "04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/id #uuid "2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/id #uuid "4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddles points at $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=0 \\\\ 2y-x=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]}] "assertions" [["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/id #uuid "3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793"] ["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/math-academy-id "q-103946"] ["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.$"] ["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})} & -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}(1-2{x}^{2}){e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0 \\\\ -2xy{e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}1-2{x}^{2}=0 \\\\ -2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n1-2{x}^{2}=0\\,⟹\\,x=\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\n \n\nSubstituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-(\\pm \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})y=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."] ["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411"] ["answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" :answer/id #uuid "ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8"] ["answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8" :answer/value "(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)"] ["answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" :answer/id #uuid "0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815"] ["answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815" :answer/value "(\\sqrt{2},1)"] ["answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" :answer/id #uuid "ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b"] ["answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b" :answer/value "$(2,0)$ and $(-2,0)$"] ["answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer/id #uuid "3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4"] ["answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4" :answer/value "$(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$ and $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},0)$"] ["answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64" :answer/id #uuid "e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64"] ["answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64" :answer/value "$(\\sqrt{2},1)$ and $(-\\sqrt{2},-1)$"] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/correct "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4"] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/choices "answer-ab75a4fc-2e7f-5073-8208-5bb2a1704cd8"] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/choices "answer-0ccb0a46-7f08-564c-91f7-36ac15556815"] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/choices "answer-ec041ce7-4b13-5a33-befa-4c27a0b8b07b"] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/choices "answer-3afe7dca-912b-5e49-87ff-b074e745dad4"] ["answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411" :answer-field/choices "answer-e5e0f804-7571-501b-87e2-55aaee4fef64"] ["question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-6b84b635-e2f3-5571-acbc-aee2c5448411"] [17592186124653 :knowledge-point/questions "question-3e112cc1-03f8-5d0c-87ca-e5043bf5d793"] ["question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/id #uuid "8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe"] ["question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-103926"] ["question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/problem "Determine the critical points of $f(x,y)=x{y}^{2}-4x+8y.$"] ["question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/worked-solution "The critical points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(a)$ does not exist, where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & ={y}^{2}-4 \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2xy+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4 & 2xy+8\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points, we need to find the points $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}$ such that ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}$ or ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ does not exist. Note that, in our case, ${f}^{′}(x,y)$ exists everywhere, so we just need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\n\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ 2xy+8=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n2xy+8=0\\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{y}.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $y$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into $x=-\\frac{4}{y},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{(-2)}=2 \\\\ y=2 & \\,⟹\\,x=-\\frac{4}{2}=-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has two solutions, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has two critical points, $(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2).$ Note that since ${f}^{′}(x)={0}^{T}$ at these two points, they are also both stationary points."] ["question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/id #uuid "a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892"] ["answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" :answer/id #uuid "372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668"] ["answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668" :answer/value "(4,4)"] ["answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" :answer/id #uuid "00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003"] ["answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003" :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ $"] ["answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer/id #uuid "21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d"] ["answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d" :answer/value "$(2,-2)$ and $(-2,2)$"] ["answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" :answer/id #uuid "8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6"] ["answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6" :answer/value "$(2,2)$ and $(-2,-2)$"] ["answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8" :answer/id #uuid "769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8"] ["answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8" :answer/value "$(4,-2)$ and $(-4,2)$"] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/correct "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d"] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/choices "answer-372af7e2-d50e-5e72-a499-a308d43de668"] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/choices "answer-00f64e4d-81e4-5c72-84a2-8f9d4f4b7003"] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/choices "answer-21293cc9-ef4b-5127-9c54-f35b9ae4554d"] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/choices "answer-8cb7f350-b88e-5213-8cd6-2c223d8a97e6"] ["answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892" :answer-field/choices "answer-769e3ce2-5299-535a-a0be-e380fc4706e8"] ["question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-a60f7d70-b0fc-5169-a4bc-8635f6350892"] [17592186124653 :knowledge-point/questions "question-8d70bf0e-d43a-5715-a8f9-251ae990c8fe"] ["question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/id #uuid "c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e"] ["question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-36677"] ["question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)={e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.$"] ["question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} & 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0 \\\\ 2y{e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\ne 0$ for any point $(x,y)\\in {R}^{2},$ we must have \n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=0 \\\\ y=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(0,0).$"] ["question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/id #uuid "9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c"] ["answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer/id #uuid "e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc"] ["answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc" :answer/value "(0,0)"] ["answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" :answer/id #uuid "0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c"] ["answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c" :answer/value "(0,2)"] ["answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" :answer/id #uuid "39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec"] ["answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec" :answer/value "(2,0)"] ["answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" :answer/id #uuid "12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182"] ["answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182" :answer/value "(2,2)"] ["answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3" :answer/id #uuid "002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3"] ["answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3" :answer/value "No stationary points"] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/correct "answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc"] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/choices "answer-e5993b94-e08d-5156-ba17-8c6f8836c6bc"] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/choices "answer-0749c3d7-da1b-587a-a3df-69d235451d3c"] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/choices "answer-39259c10-6efb-5a51-b653-92f9766862ec"] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/choices "answer-12398915-d63f-5e27-afdb-be1f6db70182"] ["answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c" :answer-field/choices "answer-002b97a2-a6b7-5cd7-9616-af11e4231fd3"] ["question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-9ae32f88-30e5-5210-86d4-1850ad11001c"] [17592186124653 :knowledge-point/questions "question-c0460f02-5116-53e0-8333-fa6ec98b4c5e"] ["question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/id #uuid "524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977"] ["question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/math-academy-id "q-36799"] ["question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/problem "Determine the stationary point of $f(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+4xy+{y}^{2}+4y.$"] ["question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/worked-solution "The stationary points of a multivariable function $f$ are the points $a$ in the domain of $f$ such that ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T},$ where\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(a)={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{1}} & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{2}} & ⋯ & \\frac{∂f}{∂{x}_{n}}\\end{aligned}]|}_{x=a}.\n$$\n\nIn tihs case, the domain of $f$ is ${R}^{2},$ and the first partial derivatives of $f$ are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =4x+4y \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =4x+2y+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first derivative of $f$ is\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}4x+4y & 4x+2y+4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the stationary points, we need to find the points where ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T}.$\nSetting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we have the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x+4y=0 \\\\ 4x+2y+4=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for $x$ in the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n4x+4y=0\\,⟹\\,x=-y.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting $x=-y$ into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(-y)+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -4y+2y+4 & =0 \\\\ -2y & =-4 \\\\ y & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBack-substituting this value into $x=-y,$ we obtain\n\n$$\ny=2\\,⟹\\,x=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our system has one solution, $(-2,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f$ has one stationary point, $(-2,2).$"] ["question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/id #uuid "a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8"] ["answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/id #uuid "1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2"] ["answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2" :answer/value "(-2,2)"] ["answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/id #uuid "89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d"] ["answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d" :answer/value "(0,4)"] ["answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/id #uuid "878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e"] ["answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e" :answer/value "(2,2)"] ["answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/id #uuid "32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b"] ["answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/type :answer.type/math] ["answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b" :answer/value "(4,0)"] ["answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/id #uuid "7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726"] ["answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726" :answer/value "No stationary points"] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/correct "answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2"] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-1af52b2f-5397-5a81-abc1-978645b4d1a2"] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-89248e74-a748-5084-86b6-3604e1ebdb8d"] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-878c880e-3b54-584e-b46c-b726a887844e"] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-32a95250-bdf5-5705-8ecc-b001eba9f20b"] ["answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8" :answer-field/choices "answer-7eca7f5a-6e66-5afe-a187-bb1fac895726"] ["question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-a7580df7-3ea9-5eba-8784-550ab709f1c8"] [17592186124653 :knowledge-point/questions "question-524ccb21-d45d-53ab-aaf4-d88936372977"] ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/id #uuid "1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43"] ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/math-academy-id "q-105280"] ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/problem "Find and categorize all the local extrema of the function \n$f(x,y)$ \ngiven that\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\n$$"] ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, notice that since ${f}^{′}(x,y)=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & -2y{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}],$ we must have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂x}={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y{e}^{x}.\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting ${f}^{′}(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2})=0 \\\\ -2y{e}^{x}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the second equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n-2y{e}^{x}=0\\,⟹\\,y=0.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting this into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{0}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+2x & =0 \\\\ x(x+2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))={e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2)\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2y{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({e}^{x}({x}^{2}+2x-{y}^{2}))=-2y{e}^{x}\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2y{e}^{x})=-2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x}({x}^{2}+4x-{y}^{2}+2) & -2y{e}^{x} \\\\ -2y{e}^{x} & -2{e}^{x}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0)=det({f}^{″}(0,0))=2\\cdot (-2)-{0}^{2}=-4<0,\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(0,0)$ is a saddle point.\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-2,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-2,0)=[\\begin{aligned}-2{e}^{-2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2{e}^{-2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-2,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-2,0)=det({f}^{″}(-2,0))=(-2{e}^{-2})^{2}-{0}^{2}=4{e}^{-4}>0\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(-2,0)>0$ and $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(-2,0)=({f}^{″}(-2,0))_{11}=-2{e}^{-2}<0,$ the function $f$ has a local maximum at $(-2,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0).$"] ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/id #uuid "a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20"] ["answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/id #uuid "cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7"] ["answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7" :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a local maximum at $(1,0)$"] ["answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/id #uuid "8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f"] ["answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f" :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(-1,0)$ and a saddle point at $(1,0)$"] ["answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/id #uuid "04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3"] ["answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3" :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$"] ["answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/id #uuid "2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e"] ["answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e" :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(0,0)$ and a local maximum at $(-2,0)$"] ["answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/id #uuid "4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6"] ["answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/type :answer.type/text] ["answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6" :answer/value "$f(x,y)$ has saddles points at $(0,0)$ and $(-2,0)$"] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/key "selection"] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/correct "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e"] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/choices "answer-cda095a0-bf38-5307-9122-32f99a1602b7"] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/choices "answer-8002492d-5e55-5026-af75-c4385bd4e90f"] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/choices "answer-04ac1472-02e4-5f9b-96d9-0b4fd8a918e3"] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/choices "answer-2193002c-a5f9-5f43-9491-8ce090c6ce8e"] ["answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20" :answer-field/choices "answer-4493e258-0fda-5c69-96f7-243298d2e5b6"] ["question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43" :question/answer-fields "answer-field-a2a97630-0fc1-580b-b276-59f04f851b20"] [17592186124655 :knowledge-point/questions "question-1640e015-4c92-5aff-b3e3-71cc75c19c43"] [17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=0 \\\\ 2y-x=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=[\\begin{aligned}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"]] "retractions" [[17592186125891 :tutorial/content "The **second partial derivatives test** helps to determine whether a stationary point of a function of two variables gives rise to a local maximum, a local minimum, or a saddle point.\n\nSuppose that $\\mathbf x = \\mathbf{a}$ is a critical point of $f(\\mathbf x),$ and let $H(\\mathbf{a})$ denote the determinant of $\\boldsymbol f''(x,y)$ evaluated at $\\mathbf{a}$ (recall that $H(\\mathbf a)$ is called the *Hessian determinant* of $f$ at $\\mathbf a$).\n\nThe second partial derivative test states the following:\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$  then $f$ has a local maximum at $\\mathbf{a},$ and\n\n- if $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(\\mathbf{a}) > 0,$  then $f$ has a local minimum at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) < 0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $\\mathbf{a}.$\n\n- If $H(\\mathbf{a}) = 0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive, and we need to classify the point using some other method.\n\nAs an example, consider the function $f (x, y) = 3 + 2x^2 - x y + y^2.$ Let's find the critical points of this function and then classify them using the second partial derivatives test.\n\nWe start by computing the partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = 4x -y, \\qquad \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y} = 2y -x\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $\\boldsymbol f'(x,y) = \\mathbf{0}^T,$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4x-y=0 \\\\ 2y-x=0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution to this system is $x=0, y=0.$ Hence, $(0,0)$ is the only stationary point.  \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4x-y)=4 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2y-x)=2 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x}=\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y-x)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(0,0),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,0)=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,0),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,0) = \\det(\\boldsymbol f''(0,0)) = 4 \\cdot 2 - (-1)^2 = 7 > 0.\n$$\n\nFinally, since $H(0,0) > 0$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial^2 f}{\\partial x^2}(0,0) = (\\boldsymbol f''(0,0))_{11}= 4 >0,$ the function $f$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has a local minimum at $(0,0).$"]]}]